  "statusText": "Mapped policy state: EA_ON. Tail risk remains in the low band with no live Nasdaq-100 price path available, while stress-side conditions stay high on the unresolved Strait of Hormuz disruption, elevated crude and rising global long yields.",
  "recommendation": "Tail remains well below the block threshold, so the deterministic mapping returns EA_ON for both NQ futures and NAS100 CFD. A narrow observation window is flagged around the Sunday CME restart, where thin liquidity and two-sided weekend headline risk are concentrated. Existing-position management and emergency protection logic are unaffected by this mapping.",
  "headline": "Nasdaq-100 still halted into the final stretch before the Sunday CME restart; tail low, stress high on unresolved Hormuz risk",
  "body": "No live Nasdaq-100 price path exists at this timestamp. CME equity index futures are still inside the weekend halt and are scheduled to restart near 22:00 UTC, roughly ninety minutes after this assessment, so the evaluation continues to rest on the last completed cash session, verified cross-asset levels and the current macro and geopolitical backdrop rather than on observed intraday structure.\n\nThe September Nasdaq-100 futures contract last traded at 30,141.75, down 0.15% on Friday inside a 30,028.50-30,283.00 range, and the cash index closed at 30,046.14, about 2.3% below its 30,762.20 52-week high while still finishing the week higher. VIX ended at 14.25 and Treasury constant-maturity yields stood at 4.15% for the 2Y, 4.63% for the 10Y and 5.21% for the 30Y at the latest H.15 publication, against a broader theme of rising long rates globally. Subdued implied volatility alongside a contained settlement range is not the profile of a market already in one-way structure.\n\nFriday's internals remain dispersed rather than directional and are treated as stress and confidence inputs, not tail evidence. Technology fell 0.46% as a sector, semiconductor equipment 1.71% and application software 1.70%, while the megacap set closed two higher and six lower with Broadcom down about 5.9%; inside semiconductors AMD rose 6.5% and Micron 2.3%. Cross-sectional dispersion of that size reduces directional conviction instead of establishing persistent one-way behaviour.\n\nThe dominant unresolved channel is still energy and geopolitics, and this hour's checks found no new escalation beyond what was already reflected. The Strait of Hormuz remains effectively disrupted with the U.S. naval blockade in place and repeated projectile strikes on Gulf shipping through the past week, including two ADNOC vessels struck on Thursday; transit volumes stay a small fraction of pre-crisis levels and the southern Omani corridor accounts for most reported incidents since early July. WTI settled near $82.40 (+1.42%) and Brent near $88.52 (+1.67%), a sixth consecutive daily advance and roughly +5% on the week. Against that, Iran and Oman describe route talks as close to complete while Tehran conditions any full reopening on sanctions relief and compensation, and Washington has signalled an indefinite blockade. The headline set is therefore two-sided rather than one-way, which is why the restart is scored as a stress and gap concentration point rather than as directional tail evidence.\n\nNo observed amplifier stack is present: there is no measurable retracement behaviour, no session extremes to hold and no opening structure to score while the market is closed. Under the fixed mapping the current tail of 15 keeps the mapped policy state at EA_ON. The week ahead is macro-light into midweek, with FOMC minutes on 19 August the first high-impact scheduled catalyst and flash PMIs on 21 August the second; the Kansas City Fed symposium falls on 27-29 August, outside this seven-day window.",
